    ('489f64e3-4967-4c14-8f69-f73c48508ca1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Covered over again to protect it, the Chuping Archaeological Site in Ren''ai Township, Nantou County, holds relics of the first prehistoric mountain settlements in Taiwan.', 'The first prehistoric mountain settlement found in its country, from the 4th millennium BC. High-altitude Neolithic farming communities are rare anywhere in the world.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:489f64e3-4967-4c14-8f69-f73c48508ca1:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The first prehistoric mountain settlement found in its country, from the 4th millennium BC. High-altitude Neolithic farming communities are rare anywhere in the world.'' restored on Chuping Archaeological Site', 'ab154f17722df554b84830f38aebfe9ec3d8b85ace902fbb54d5db7538de492f|d7407a771c83148427303f07edc1500c6b57914757eee949cc2272c93c57e90a|d7407a771c83148427303f07edc1500c6b57914757eee949cc2272c93c57e90a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Covered over again to protect it, the Chuping Archaeological Site in Ren''ai Township, Nantou County, holds relics of the first prehistoric mountain settlements in Taiwan."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site was covered over again [S5]; it was covered to protect it [S5]; the site is in Ren''ai Township [S1]; the site is in Nantou County [S1]; it holds relics of the first prehistoric mountain settlements in Taiwan [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-verify2-004 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Chuping Archaeological Site is an archaeological site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuping_Archaeological_Site]; The site is in Ren''ai Township [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuping_Archaeological_Site]; The site is in Nantou County, Taiwan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuping_Archaeological_Site]; It holds relics of prehistoric settlements [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuping_Archaeological_Site]; The settlements are mountain settlements [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuping_Archaeological_Site]; They are the first prehistoric mountain settlements in Taiwan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuping_Archaeological_Site]; The site was covered over again [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuping_Archaeological_Site]; It was covered to protect it [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuping_Archaeological_Site]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4a4937bf-ef17-43d7-b7c4-28afa1b0f230'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near the San Pedro River, 60 km west of Tikal, El Perú was the capital of a Maya city-state, where the first recorded meeting between Teotihuacan and the Maya took place in 378.', '500 BC-800 AD Maya city-state capital known as Waka'', 60 km west of Tikal near the San Pedro River. El Perú controlled a strategic Petén corridor and held political ties to major Classic-era powers.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:4a4937bf-ef17-43d7-b7c4-28afa1b0f230:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description "500 BC-800 AD Maya city-state capital known as Waka'', 60 km west of Tikal near the San Pedro River. El Perú controlled a strategic Petén corridor and held political ties to major Classic-era powers." restored on El Perú', '9a090abf492dfd57789c00224f2dbbae73354be4bea07e21818f432a1aac9baa|e371138dc851a789e3a237dea61f210da29acececdbb2d6824a6f040d6c90bae|e371138dc851a789e3a237dea61f210da29acececdbb2d6824a6f040d6c90bae', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near the San Pedro River, 60 km west of Tikal, El Perú was the capital of a Maya city-state, where the first recorded meeting between Teotihuacan and the Maya took place in 378."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is near the San Pedro River [S2]; it is 60 km west of Tikal [S3]; it is called El Perú [S1]; it was the capital of a Maya city-state [S2]; the first recorded meeting between Teotihuacan and the Maya took place there [S6]; that meeting took place in 378 [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-026 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "El Perú (Waka'') is a pre-Columbian Maya archaeological site in Guatemala and was the capital of a Maya city-state (central claim) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Per%C3%BA_(Maya_site)]; El Perú lies in Guatemala (Department of Petén) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Per%C3%BA_(Maya_site)]; El Perú lies near the San Pedro River [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Per%C3%BA_(Maya_site)]; El Perú is 60 km west of Tikal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Per%C3%BA_(Maya_site)]; The first recorded meeting between Teotihuacan and the Maya took place at El Perú (Waka'') [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Per%C3%BA_(Maya_site)]; That meeting took place in 378 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Per%C3%BA_(Maya_site)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4ab1bd6e-a7fd-41c3-b341-9959d77d5a6e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Built by King Ur-Nammu in the 21st century BC, the Great Ziggurat of Ur belonged to a temple complex of the moon god Nanna; its remains are mud brick faced with burnt bricks set in bitumen.', 'The structure was built during the Early Bronze Age by King Ur-Nammu of the Third Dynasty of Ur.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:4ab1bd6e-a7fd-41c3-b341-9959d77d5a6e:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The structure was built during the Early Bronze Age by King Ur-Nammu of the Third Dynasty of Ur.'' restored on Great Ziggurat of Ur', 'f6549d24946163c81a83d9c956bd02363309d877fff09d62701e80411265a96a|111b5153daef4f63550aeffd989fb1e6de3168c73ee52c6f87d864151de42148|111b5153daef4f63550aeffd989fb1e6de3168c73ee52c6f87d864151de42148', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Built by King Ur-Nammu in the 21st century BC, the Great Ziggurat of Ur belonged to a temple complex of the moon god Nanna; its remains are mud brick faced with burnt bricks set in bitumen."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it was built by King Ur-Nammu [S2]; it was built in the 21st century BC [S2]; it is called the Great Ziggurat of Ur [S1]; it belonged to a temple complex [S5]; the temple complex was a shrine of the moon god Nanna [S5]; its remains are mud brick [S6]; the mud brick is faced with burnt bricks [S6]; the burnt bricks are set in bitumen [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Great Ziggurat of Ur is a ziggurat (a temple tower) in the city of Ur [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ziggurat_of_Ur]; It is in Iraq (near Nasiriyah, Dhi Qar Province) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ziggurat_of_Ur]; It was built by King Ur-Nammu [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ziggurat_of_Ur]; It was built in the 21st century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ziggurat_of_Ur]; It belonged to a temple complex [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ziggurat_of_Ur]; The temple complex was a shrine of the moon god Nanna [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ziggurat_of_Ur]; Its remains are mud brick [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ziggurat_of_Ur]; The mud brick is faced with burnt bricks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ziggurat_of_Ur]; The burnt bricks are set in bitumen [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ziggurat_of_Ur]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4acf93dc-6942-41bf-90f2-107407872c9a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Between Cherchell and Algiers, the Royal Mausoleum of Mauretania once stood about 40 metres high, its base decorated with 60 Ionic columns. Its date and real function are uncertain.', 'A circular stone mausoleum built around 3 BC for the kings of Mauretania. Its round form with a pyramid top blends Berber and Hellenistic styles.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:4acf93dc-6942-41bf-90f2-107407872c9a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A circular stone mausoleum built around 3 BC for the kings of Mauretania. Its round form with a pyramid top blends Berber and Hellenistic styles.'' restored on Royal Mausoleum of Mauretania', 'd90e342ec454b1df688f0e66d5acb5bf99dbb6694b5c0b8164d36ee3fb28c7c7|364b579369c00045fd334cd259c64317fda5f510aa0b7715abcf0ce85a5cd98e|364b579369c00045fd334cd259c64317fda5f510aa0b7715abcf0ce85a5cd98e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Between Cherchell and Algiers, the Royal Mausoleum of Mauretania once stood about 40 metres high, its base decorated with 60 Ionic columns. Its date and real function are uncertain."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the monument lies between Cherchell and Algiers [S1]; it once stood about 40 metres high [S4]; its base was decorated with 60 Ionic columns [S5]; its date is uncertain [W1]; its real function is uncertain [W1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-verify2-004 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Royal Mausoleum of Mauretania is a funerary monument (a tomb) on the road between Cherchell and Algiers, in Algeria [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Mausoleum_of_Mauretania]; The monument once stood about 40 metres high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Mausoleum_of_Mauretania]; Its base was decorated with 60 Ionic columns [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Mausoleum_of_Mauretania]; The date of the monument is uncertain [SUPPORTED: https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mausol%C3%A9e_royal_de_Maur%C3%A9tanie]; The real function of the monument is uncertain [SUPPORTED: https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mausol%C3%A9e_royal_de_Maur%C3%A9tanie]"}, {"source": "web fact W1", "url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mausol%C3%A9e_royal_de_Maur%C3%A9tanie", "quote": "La date de construction et la fonction réelle de ce monument ne sont pas connues avec certitude."}]'::jsonb),
    ('4b29999c-68c7-4726-9c5a-47ea177779f4'::uuid, 'card_description', 'From Stonehenge to the River Avon runs Stonehenge Avenue, nearly 3 kilometres long, built in the period of 2600 to 1700 BCE and aligned in places with the summer solstice sunrise.', 'A 3 km avenue linking Stonehenge to the River Avon, aligned with the summer solstice sunrise. A vanished stone circle once stood at the river end.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:4b29999c-68c7-4726-9c5a-47ea177779f4:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A 3 km avenue linking Stonehenge to the River Avon, aligned with the summer solstice sunrise. A vanished stone circle once stood at the river end.'' restored on Stonehenge Avenue', '6ba72995131d5d6ba9c0726cb6899820c4fb7f5cd3d9cc7b57882901d37977f1|6da1cbef6cd63b298b78cb6b837f0625cbfe8ab63f15f6c8af3922c927901d0d|6da1cbef6cd63b298b78cb6b837f0625cbfe8ab63f15f6c8af3922c927901d0d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "From Stonehenge to the River Avon runs Stonehenge Avenue, nearly 3 kilometres long, built in the period of 2600 to 1700 BCE and aligned in places with the summer solstice sunrise."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it runs from Stonehenge to the River Avon [S3]; it is called Stonehenge Avenue [S1]; it is nearly 3 kilometres long [S3]; it was built in the period of 2600 to 1700 BCE [S4]; along part of its length it is aligned with the summer solstice sunrise [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Stonehenge Avenue is an ancient avenue on Salisbury Plain, Wiltshire, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonehenge_Avenue]; It runs from Stonehenge to the River Avon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonehenge_Avenue]; It is nearly 3 kilometres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonehenge_Avenue]; It was built in the period of 2600 to 1700 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonehenge_Avenue]; It is aligned in places with the summer solstice sunrise [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stonehenge_Avenue]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4b4a9785-3993-4bc1-91f2-6df76949b100'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At Woerden, on a natural levee by the river Oude Rijn, the Romans built Castellum Laurum on the empire''s northern border. A Frankish sword from the 8th century was found there in 2012.', 'The first castellum was built in the 40s AD and was destroyed in 69 AD during the Batavian rebellion.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:4b4a9785-3993-4bc1-91f2-6df76949b100:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The first castellum was built in the 40s AD and was destroyed in 69 AD during the Batavian rebellion.'' restored on Woerden', 'b55f511fbcf7b848cdc944e34e16abf03ac23acf705fb362d9ac3809d0dad5ea|9222134966353fa1129b05e858f0b79c43d50e03d22f1d294fd92d863521a232|9222134966353fa1129b05e858f0b79c43d50e03d22f1d294fd92d863521a232', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Woerden, on a natural levee by the river Oude Rijn, the Romans built Castellum Laurum on the empire''s northern border. A Frankish sword from the 8th century was found there in 2012."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is Woerden [S1]; it lies on a natural levee [S2, S3]; it lies by the river Oude Rijn [S1]; the Romans built Castellum Laurum there [S3]; the castellum stood on the northern border of the empire [S3]; a Frankish sword was found on the castellum site [S6]; the sword dates from the 8th century [S6]; the sword was found in 2012 [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Castellum Laurum was a Roman fort (castellum) at Woerden in the Netherlands [SUPPORTED: https://www.livius.org/articles/place/laurum-woerden/]; The Romans built the castellum at Woerden [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woerden]; The fort stood on a natural levee [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woerden]; Woerden lies on the river Oude Rijn [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woerden]; The fort stood on the northern border of the Roman Empire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woerden]; The sword is Frankish [SUPPORTED: https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zwaard_van_Woerden]; The sword is from the 8th century [SUPPORTED: https://www.stadsmuseumwoerden.nl/nieuws/frankisch-zwaard-in-het-stadsmuseum]; The sword was found in 2012 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woerden]; The sword was found at the site of the castellum [SUPPORTED: https://nl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zwaard_van_Woerden]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4ce83e01-a19d-40d0-a739-d6b571b9e824'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a terrace 300 feet above the Lycus river, Hierapolis was famous for its high quality woollen fabrics and dye products. In AD 17, under Tiberius, a major earthquake destroyed the city.', 'Built over a Phrygian cult center of the goddess Cybele, this Greco-Roman city sits atop hot springs that create surreal white travertine terraces.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:4ce83e01-a19d-40d0-a739-d6b571b9e824:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Built over a Phrygian cult center of the goddess Cybele, this Greco-Roman city sits atop hot springs that create surreal white travertine terraces.'' restored on Hierapolis', 'b2a85cada6bfbc56589364e10d86e5478e52f2f4d863b83254e68852c80220e1|bd29c8abc067ab1dbf03fe5be823946465c0265458e46249c305508e9ebac106|bd29c8abc067ab1dbf03fe5be823946465c0265458e46249c305508e9ebac106', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a terrace 300 feet above the Lycus river, Hierapolis was famous for its high quality woollen fabrics and dye products. In AD 17, under Tiberius, a major earthquake destroyed the city."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the city lies on a terrace 300 feet above the Lycus river [S1]; the city is called Hierapolis [S1]; it was famous for high quality woollen fabrics [S4]; it was famous for dye products [S4]; in AD 17 a major earthquake destroyed the city [S6]; this happened under the emperor Tiberius [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Hierapolis was a city (a Hellenistic Greek city in Phrygia) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierapolis]; It is in Turkey [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierapolis]; It lay on a terrace above the Lycus river [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierapolis]; The terrace is 300 feet above the river [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierapolis]; It was famous for its high quality woollen fabrics [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierapolis]; It was famous for its dye products [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierapolis]; The earthquake came in AD 17 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierapolis]; The earthquake came under Tiberius [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierapolis]; A major earthquake destroyed the city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierapolis]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4d70c735-5eb1-4907-a2a3-9c25871a951b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Stane Street, a Roman road of 91 km from London to Chichester, was in use by 70 AD. For much of its length it had a central agger with parallel ditches either side.', 'A 91 km road linking Londinium to Chichester, in use by 70 AD. Construction may date to within the first decade of the Roman occupation, as early as 43–53 AD.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:4d70c735-5eb1-4907-a2a3-9c25871a951b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A 91 km road linking Londinium to Chichester, in use by 70 AD. Construction may date to within the first decade of the Roman occupation, as early as 43–53 AD.'' restored on Stane Street, Chichester', 'b515653018dff4fa623a96286fe78baff04d8f3b1554801397f36179bea965e4|acfd33f70c99199b1d09cee67c4edd7df695a18d50dbb1fd4a4372a3181751b3|acfd33f70c99199b1d09cee67c4edd7df695a18d50dbb1fd4a4372a3181751b3', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-rewrite1-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Stane Street, a Roman road of 91 km from London to Chichester, was in use by 70 AD. For much of its length it had a central agger with parallel ditches either side."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check1-001 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Stane Street is a Roman road [S1]; it is 91 km long [S1]; it runs from London to Chichester [S1]; it was in use by 70 AD [S2]; for much of its length it had a central agger [S5]; for much of its length it had parallel ditches either side [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-028 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Stane Street is a Roman road (the central claim: a road, not a building or settlement) in southern England. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stane_Street_(Chichester)]; The road ran from London to Chichester. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stane_Street_(Chichester)]; The road is 91 km long. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stane_Street_(Chichester)]; The road was in use by 70 AD. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stane_Street_(Chichester)]; For much of its length the road had a central agger (raised embankment). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stane_Street_(Chichester)]; For much of its length the road had parallel ditches either side of the agger. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stane_Street_(Chichester)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4e9fb2b3-94fe-4898-80c8-6678ac2d5903'::uuid, 'card_description', 'People have been buried at Gamla Uppsala for 2,000 years, a place famed as the residence of the legendary Yngling kings. Of between 2,000 and 3,000 mounds, only 250 barrows remain.', 'The legendary seat of the Yngling kings from the 3rd century AD onward, famous across Scandinavia. Three massive royal burial mounds still dominate the landscape.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:4e9fb2b3-94fe-4898-80c8-6678ac2d5903:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The legendary seat of the Yngling kings from the 3rd century AD onward, famous across Scandinavia. Three massive royal burial mounds still dominate the landscape.'' restored on Gamla Uppsala', '504ac20a42e361020b53d0ff9b9e53d795373c8efefc0b31974e84a2130e0219|c864ce8fa1d39617ddbf578f1dbe1b3a67c51e2724fb776c8bfc04b28f9c3ee0|c864ce8fa1d39617ddbf578f1dbe1b3a67c51e2724fb776c8bfc04b28f9c3ee0', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "People have been buried at Gamla Uppsala for 2,000 years, a place famed as the residence of the legendary Yngling kings. Of between 2,000 and 3,000 mounds, only 250 barrows remain."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "people have been buried at Gamla Uppsala for 2,000 years [S2]; Gamla Uppsala was famed as the residence of kings [S1]; the kings were of the legendary Yngling dynasty [S1]; there were originally between 2,000 and 3,000 mounds [S3]; only 250 barrows remain [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-028 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gamla Uppsala is a burial place (mounds and barrows) at a village/parish outside Uppsala in Sweden (the central claim). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamla_Uppsala]; People have been buried at Gamla Uppsala for 2,000 years. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamla_Uppsala]; The place was famed as the residence of the legendary Yngling kings. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamla_Uppsala]; There were originally between 2,000 and 3,000 mounds. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamla_Uppsala]; Only 250 barrows remain today. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamla_Uppsala]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('4f522637-ca21-4ac0-b2f3-3edf8e258e1c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the centre of Sofia, the Amphitheatre of Serdica rose on a theatre ravaged by the Goths. Built for up to around 25,000, its ruins yielded a rare bronze medallion of Antinous.', 'The amphitheatre was built in the 3rd–4th century AD on top of a 2nd–3rd century theatre, which had been ravaged by the Goths.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:4f522637-ca21-4ac0-b2f3-3edf8e258e1c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The amphitheatre was built in the 3rd–4th century AD on top of a 2nd–3rd century theatre, which had been ravaged by the Goths.'' restored on Amphitheatre of Serdica', '23cd7ce75af15379a3307654c416ebcea6afd7cfbc3cad0a4f5722099dc94540|33d7618239b3be6e208c3aa07caee16f11da891dd90f546061184794933a620b|33d7618239b3be6e208c3aa07caee16f11da891dd90f546061184794933a620b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the centre of Sofia, the Amphitheatre of Serdica rose on a theatre ravaged by the Goths. Built for up to around 25,000, its ruins yielded a rare bronze medallion of Antinous."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the amphitheatre ruins lie in the centre of Sofia [S1, S2]; the amphitheatre was built on top of a theatre [S3, S5]; the theatre had been ravaged by the Goths [S3]; the amphitheatre was designed for up to around 25,000 people [S6]; the site yielded a rare bronze medallion of Antinous [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-028 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Amphitheatre of Serdica is a Roman amphitheatre (the central claim: an arena for gladiator and beast fights, in ancient Serdica). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphitheatre_of_Serdica]; The ruins lie in the centre of Sofia. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphitheatre_of_Serdica]; The amphitheatre was built on top of an earlier theatre. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphitheatre_of_Serdica]; That earlier theatre had been ravaged by the Goths. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphitheatre_of_Serdica]; It was built for up to around 25,000 spectators. [SUPPORTED: https://www.roamintheempire.com/index.php/2021/05/05/serdica-part-i/]; The ruins yielded a rare bronze medallion of Antinous. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amphitheatre_of_Serdica]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('50abd026-06c4-4790-a102-26da88b3eae4'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Wéris Megaliths are scattered over more than 5 miles. In two surviving dolmens of conglomerate, a stone slab pierced with a circular opening separates the chamber from the ante-chamber.', 'A megalithic site stretching over 8 km near the village of Weris, unique in its country. Two well-preserved dolmens and numerous menhirs date to around 3000 BC.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:50abd026-06c4-4790-a102-26da88b3eae4:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A megalithic site stretching over 8 km near the village of Weris, unique in its country. Two well-preserved dolmens and numerous menhirs date to around 3000 BC.'' restored on Wéris Megaliths', '401aa7a4ed71a62b3a0045ada1bf3ba05cc8618324a3dc38cab272af5ff66c59|a89ffdaf04cc253e45ec615f2ca7f9e0e499012ee48f6d33a7884f206905a669|a89ffdaf04cc253e45ec615f2ca7f9e0e499012ee48f6d33a7884f206905a669', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Wéris Megaliths are scattered over more than 5 miles. In two surviving dolmens of conglomerate, a stone slab pierced with a circular opening separates the chamber from the ante-chamber."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the megalithic remains at Wéris are scattered over more than 5 miles [S2]; two dolmens survive [S4]; the dolmens are made of conglomerate [S6]; a stone slab pierced with a circular opening separates the funerary chamber from the ante-chamber [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-028 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Wéris Megaliths are a group of megalithic monuments (dolmens and menhirs) near the village of Wéris, Belgium (the central claim). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C3%A9ris_megaliths]; The megaliths are scattered over a region more than 5 miles long. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C3%A9ris_megaliths]; Two dolmens survive. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C3%A9ris_megaliths]; The dolmens are made of conglomerate. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C3%A9ris_megaliths]; In the two dolmens a stone slab pierced with a circular opening separates the chamber from the ante-chamber. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C3%A9ris_megaliths]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('51e00207-24c0-4f44-b581-4d8584df5296'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Set on the slope of La Concepción hill so it could be seen from the sea, the Roman Theatre of Cartagena seated some 6,000. After a fire, its remains were dismantled to build a market.', 'The Theater, inaugurated in 1 B.C., functioned as such for two centuries, although partial restorations were necessary during that time.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:51e00207-24c0-4f44-b581-4d8584df5296:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The Theater, inaugurated in 1 B.C., functioned as such for two centuries, although partial restorations were necessary during that time.'' restored on Roman Theatre, Cartagena', '237f73a865faee22a5e0c204ba453d034e3e716c3315c5e8f0beefb43457ffb1|41280ab90cb7838eca68cdc65629feff88b01de3962ba61b2a69407e1376b17b|41280ab90cb7838eca68cdc65629feff88b01de3962ba61b2a69407e1376b17b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Set on the slope of La Concepción hill so it could be seen from the sea, the Roman Theatre of Cartagena seated some 6,000. After a fire, its remains were dismantled to build a market."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the theatre is set on the slope of the hill of La Concepción [S3]; it was built so that it could be seen from the sea [S3]; it seated some 6,000 spectators [S2]; after a fire, what was left of the theatre was dismantled [S6]; the remains were dismantled to build a market [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-028 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Theatre of Cartagena is a Roman theatre in Carthago Nova, present-day Cartagena, Spain (the central claim). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theatre_(Cartagena)]; It was set on the slope of La Concepción hill. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theatre_(Cartagena)]; It was sited so that it could be seen from the sea. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theatre_(Cartagena)]; The theatre seated some 6,000. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theatre_(Cartagena)]; A fire destroyed much of the theatre''s structure. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theatre_(Cartagena)]; After the fire, its remains were dismantled and the elements used to build a market. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theatre_(Cartagena)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('52fe4aef-2fab-4d5b-9a5c-19b7f491a7fb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Just below the hilltop of El Castillón, above the River Esla, a shelter holds red paintings on quartzite of human-like icons, fingers and anchor-shaped symbols, more than 3000 years old.', 'The site has been inhabited since the Iron Age, but the most known period of the site belongs to the Late Roman Era as a large number of late Hispano-Roman red gloss pottery testifies.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:52fe4aef-2fab-4d5b-9a5c-19b7f491a7fb:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The site has been inhabited since the Iron Age, but the most known period of the site belongs to the Late Roman Era as a large number of late Hispano-Roman red gloss pottery testifies.'' restored on El Castillón', '47760cf357d2196ffaf4e484242d31ccbeede8b47e12322459dad8cfaa2da441|46de4d9aab36739fbb66819661135208023c00edf343cc1bb7ebaa821f8ed453|46de4d9aab36739fbb66819661135208023c00edf343cc1bb7ebaa821f8ed453', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Just below the hilltop of El Castillón, above the River Esla, a shelter holds red paintings on quartzite of human-like icons, fingers and anchor-shaped symbols, more than 3000 years old."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "a shelter lies just below the hilltop site of El Castillón [S2, S5]; the hill is above the River Esla [S2]; the shelter holds paintings in red [S5, S7]; the paintings are on quartzite [S7]; they show human-like icons [S6]; they show fingers [S6]; they show anchor-shaped symbols [S6]; the paintings are more than 3000 years old [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-verify-029 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "El Castillón is a hilltop archaeological site in Spain with a rock-art shelter just below it (central claim: a painted rock shelter below the hilltop) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castill%C3%B3n]; The site lies on a hilltop above the River Esla [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castill%C3%B3n]; The shelter lies just below the hilltop site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castill%C3%B3n]; The shelter holds paintings (schematic rock art) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castill%C3%B3n]; The paintings are red [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castill%C3%B3n]; The paintings are on quartzite [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castill%C3%B3n]; The paintings include human-like icons [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castill%C3%B3n]; The paintings include fingers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castill%C3%B3n]; The paintings include anchor-shaped symbols [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castill%C3%B3n]; The paintings are more than 3000 years old [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Castill%C3%B3n]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('53058d01-7e64-49c7-8dec-2b352a0e9ec6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In 1820 a tenant farmer toppled and buried all but one stone of Grey Croft Stone Circle. In 1949 excavators found a central cairn, and under it a broken early Bronze Age jet or lignite ring.', 'Stone circle raised before 4300 BC, toppled by a farmer in 1820, re-erected in 1949 just 600 metres from Sellafield. Ten of twelve original stones still stand near the Cumbrian coast.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:53058d01-7e64-49c7-8dec-2b352a0e9ec6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Stone circle raised before 4300 BC, toppled by a farmer in 1820, re-erected in 1949 just 600 metres from Sellafield. Ten of twelve original stones still stand near the Cumbrian coast.'' restored on Grey Croft Stone Circle', '222e45006e2e3316fc7ab83f296fa6e6d18cb48dc87b3298a18eb0144ae64257|fbc8b8cf667b7b5abb6ecdfe3064d595a2e193b155e257792cec2582b970131f|fbc8b8cf667b7b5abb6ecdfe3064d595a2e193b155e257792cec2582b970131f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1820 a tenant farmer toppled and buried all but one stone of Grey Croft Stone Circle. In 1949 excavators found a central cairn, and under it a broken early Bronze Age jet or lignite ring."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "in 1820 a tenant farmer toppled and buried the stones of the circle [S4]; all but one stone was toppled and buried [S4]; in 1949 excavators found a cairn in the centre of the circle [S5, S6]; under the cairn lay a broken early Bronze Age jet or lignite ring [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-029 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Grey Croft Stone Circle is a stone circle (a restored prehistoric stone circle near Seascale in Cumbria, England) - central claim [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Croft_stone_circle]; In 1820 the stones of the circle were toppled [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Croft_stone_circle]; The stones were buried in 1820 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Croft_stone_circle]; The toppling and burying was done by a tenant farmer [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Croft_stone_circle]; All but one stone was toppled and buried [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Croft_stone_circle]; In 1949 excavators worked at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Croft_stone_circle]; The excavation found a central cairn [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Croft_stone_circle]; Under the cairn lay a jet or lignite ring [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Croft_stone_circle]; The ring was broken [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Croft_stone_circle]; The ring is early Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Croft_stone_circle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('532df28e-27f6-4391-8167-bc139f303b3b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Honcopampa, a name possibly from the Quechua for swamp and plain, lies at about 3,500 metres in the Ancash Region, considered one of the most important sites of the Callejón de Huaylas.', 'At 3,500 metres in the Ancash Callejón de Huaylas, this Wari-culture site has never been fully mapped. Its extent across the highland terrain remains incompletely documented.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:532df28e-27f6-4391-8167-bc139f303b3b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''At 3,500 metres in the Ancash Callejón de Huaylas, this Wari-culture site has never been fully mapped. Its extent across the highland terrain remains incompletely documented.'' restored on Honcopampa', '84e26034c0344670df6cf225531185dd2dc98af340a5281b5cb8df3aa1d21fdb|6639ed8103a3d2fefb74d25ceac99fb8b3b1758b485a041574fe6406766ccedc|6639ed8103a3d2fefb74d25ceac99fb8b3b1758b485a041574fe6406766ccedc', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Honcopampa, a name possibly from the Quechua for swamp and plain, lies at about 3,500 metres in the Ancash Region, considered one of the most important sites of the Callejón de Huaylas."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the name is possibly from Quechua [S1]; the Quechua words mean swamp and plain [S1]; the site lies at about 3,500 metres [S2]; the site is in the Ancash Region [S2]; it is considered one of the most important sites of the Callejón de Huaylas [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-029 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Honcopampa is an archaeological site in Peru (central claim: a site, not a modern town) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honcopampa]; The name Honcopampa possibly comes from Quechua [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honcopampa]; The Quechua words mean swamp and plain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honcopampa]; Honcopampa lies at about 3,500 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honcopampa]; Honcopampa lies in the Ancash Region [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honcopampa]; Honcopampa is considered one of the most important sites of the Callejon de Huaylas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honcopampa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('53a9ddce-054f-4471-94fd-6243611ae0a8'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Cholesbury Camp, an Iron Age hill fort of 15 acres, may have been a refuge in times of strife. Within it lay a clay-lined oven, bloomery slag and hearths that appear to have smelted iron.', 'A well-preserved Iron Age hillfort covering 6.1 hectares with deep ramparts. A medieval church was later built right inside the ancient enclosure.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:53a9ddce-054f-4471-94fd-6243611ae0a8:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A well-preserved Iron Age hillfort covering 6.1 hectares with deep ramparts. A medieval church was later built right inside the ancient enclosure.'' restored on Cholesbury Camp', 'f1c10feb55b6079201c4cd2c6571749e345efcc902235abb689e6dc462e9fa14|517310425c1cdd88c98c7dc6c7d7317eefd54392a72e13a82f6a9ca6f9e1c914|517310425c1cdd88c98c7dc6c7d7317eefd54392a72e13a82f6a9ca6f9e1c914', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cholesbury Camp, an Iron Age hill fort of 15 acres, may have been a refuge in times of strife. Within it lay a clay-lined oven, bloomery slag and hearths that appear to have smelted iron."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cholesbury Camp is an Iron Age hill fort [S1]; it covers 15 acres [S2]; it may have been a refuge in times of strife [S4]; a clay-lined oven was found within it [S5]; bloomery slag was found [S6]; hearths were found within it [S5]; some hearths appear to have been used to smelt iron [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-029 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cholesbury Camp is an Iron Age hill fort (in Buckinghamshire, England) - central claim [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholesbury_Camp]; The hill fort covers 15 acres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholesbury_Camp]; The fort may have been a refuge in times of strife [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholesbury_Camp]; The remains of a clay-lined oven were found within the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholesbury_Camp]; Hearths (fire-sites) were found within the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholesbury_Camp]; Bloomery slag was found at the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholesbury_Camp]; Hearths within the fort appear to have been used to smelt iron [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholesbury_Camp]; The refuge/strife use was during the Iron Age (the fort was only sparsely or intermittently inhabited then) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cholesbury_Camp]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('557a6dcf-0a4a-4b76-8fed-e8f89cd2b19e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Valverde, the Great Dolmen of Zambujeiro roofs its funerary chamber with large granite slabs. Excavations in 1965 unearthed slate tablets, necklaces, crosiers and copper objects.', 'Research has dated this dolmen structure to between 4000-3000 BC, concurrent with the megalithic construction associated with the region of Évora.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:557a6dcf-0a4a-4b76-8fed-e8f89cd2b19e:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Research has dated this dolmen structure to between 4000-3000 BC, concurrent with the megalithic construction associated with the region of Évora.'' restored on Great Dolmen of Zambujeiro', '6803fa2a4f91c715a2ab84b3c6508003c28ea4d7f696f335ca87c716eea12ceb|8b8a5219fd20a10eaed566e3e63af941633ccc0cbf299f4f9d9f0e3623c6e280|8b8a5219fd20a10eaed566e3e63af941633ccc0cbf299f4f9d9f0e3623c6e280', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Valverde, the Great Dolmen of Zambujeiro roofs its funerary chamber with large granite slabs. Excavations in 1965 unearthed slate tablets, necklaces, crosiers and copper objects."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the dolmen is near Valverde [S1]; large granite slabs cover the funerary chamber [S5]; excavations took place in 1965 [S3]; they unearthed slate tablets [S4]; they unearthed necklaces [S4]; they unearthed crosiers [S4]; they unearthed copper objects [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-029 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Great Dolmen of Zambujeiro is a megalithic dolmen (a funerary monument with a chamber) in Portugal - central claim [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Dolmen_of_Zambujeiro]; The dolmen lies near Valverde [SUPPORTED: https://arqueologia.patrimoniocultural.pt/index.php/noservice.php?sid=visitaveis&subsid=47550&lang=en]; The monument has a funerary chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Dolmen_of_Zambujeiro]; The funerary chamber is covered (roofed) with large granite slabs - as built; the heritage record notes the main capstone now lies displaced on the west side of the structure [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Dolmen_of_Zambujeiro]; Excavations took place in 1965 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Dolmen_of_Zambujeiro]; The 1965 excavations unearthed slate tablets [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Dolmen_of_Zambujeiro]; The 1965 excavations unearthed necklaces [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Dolmen_of_Zambujeiro]; The 1965 excavations unearthed crosiers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Dolmen_of_Zambujeiro]; The 1965 excavations unearthed copper objects [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Dolmen_of_Zambujeiro]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('55da0a74-b7fc-4b66-a104-e6b32df0e8e3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Carved very unusually into a vertical red sandstone cliff, the Ballochmyle Cup and Ring marks spread over two panels of several hundred glyphs, among them ringed stars and squares with cups.', 'Neolithic cup-and-ring rock carvings from the 4th-2nd millennium BC. These spiral motifs appear across Atlantic Europe, but no one has decoded what they mean.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:55da0a74-b7fc-4b66-a104-e6b32df0e8e3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Neolithic cup-and-ring rock carvings from the 4th-2nd millennium BC. These spiral motifs appear across Atlantic Europe, but no one has decoded what they mean.'' restored on Ballochmyle Cup and Ring marks', 'e693dd7a1677dd8432e5640348d80cef7d695e7754ce9a27f4e51a03c27e354f|48d090147c28f4da769cb2b1ed5f2c900dc3b2d2f5bb8085449df3d65ff1fbd8|48d090147c28f4da769cb2b1ed5f2c900dc3b2d2f5bb8085449df3d65ff1fbd8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Carved very unusually into a vertical red sandstone cliff, the Ballochmyle Cup and Ring marks spread over two panels of several hundred glyphs, among them ringed stars and squares with cups."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the marks are carved very unusually into a cliff [S1]; the cliff is vertical and of red sandstone [S1]; the marks form two panels [S3]; the panels hold several hundred glyphs [S3]; ringed stars are among the glyphs [S4]; squares with cups are among the glyphs [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Ballochmyle Cup and Ring marks are a group of prehistoric rock carvings (cup and ring marks) at Ballochmyle, Mauchline, East Ayrshire, Scotland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballochmyle_cup_and_ring_marks]; The carvings are made very unusually into a vertical red sandstone cliff [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballochmyle_cup_and_ring_marks]; The carvings spread over two panels [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballochmyle_cup_and_ring_marks]; The two panels hold several hundred glyphs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballochmyle_cup_and_ring_marks]; Among the glyphs are ringed stars [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballochmyle_cup_and_ring_marks]; Among the glyphs are squares with cups [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballochmyle_cup_and_ring_marks]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('56686df2-f3f4-468a-83b8-6a3ae164b9f6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Vertillum began as a Gallic oppidum and grew into a Gallo-Roman town of perhaps 3,000 to 5,000 people, a center for working copper-based alloys, with remains of ramparts, baths and a temple.', 'A Gallo-Roman settlement covering 25 hectares with ramparts, baths, and a temple. A stone wall with gates enclosed the original Gallic oppidum.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:56686df2-f3f4-468a-83b8-6a3ae164b9f6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A Gallo-Roman settlement covering 25 hectares with ramparts, baths, and a temple. A stone wall with gates enclosed the original Gallic oppidum.'' restored on Vertillum', '3cdcaa05c3486d85a7c7abb167b230158c3436e965d4bdf06619f8a04045b3aa|2772be57175d11568971641052d4df517a0a60c7e98a4d6321406430fb733508|2772be57175d11568971641052d4df517a0a60c7e98a4d6321406430fb733508', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-rewrite1-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Vertillum began as a Gallic oppidum and grew into a Gallo-Roman town of perhaps 3,000 to 5,000 people, a center for working copper-based alloys, with remains of ramparts, baths and a temple."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check1-001 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Vertillum began as a Gallic oppidum [S4]; it grew into a Gallo-Roman town [S5]; the Roman town perhaps had 3,000 to 5,000 inhabitants (hedged) [S6]; it was a center for working copper-based alloys [S7]; remains of ramparts exist at the site [S3]; remains of baths exist at the site [S3, S5]; remains of a temple exist at the site [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Vertillum is a settlement (a Gallo-Roman town) in France [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vertillum]; Vertillum began as a Gallic oppidum [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vertillum]; It grew into a Gallo-Roman town [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vertillum]; The Roman town had perhaps 3,000 to 5,000 people [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vertillum]; It was a center for working copper-based alloys [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vertillum]; Remains of ramparts survive at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vertillum]; Remains of baths survive at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vertillum]; Remains of a temple survive at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vertillum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('58175827-2670-44ef-8eda-609467be08f1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Around Huayna Picchu the Urubamba River bends. The Incas built a trail up its side to temples and terraces at its top, 2,693 metres above sea level and high over Machu Picchu.', 'The mountain towering 260 metres above Machu Picchu, with Inca temples and terraces on its peak. The steep trail to the summit is a sacred ascent.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:58175827-2670-44ef-8eda-609467be08f1:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The mountain towering 260 metres above Machu Picchu, with Inca temples and terraces on its peak. The steep trail to the summit is a sacred ascent.'' restored on Huayna Picchu', '9179a978ed3e1edc2b8b9e1642a1965fd5f4fb0a0374ff710896c0726f8916ad|e9e0fb7db4a5925a67e945bde04be7d564070806fddeb89231c9d81ec55ba8a4|e9e0fb7db4a5925a67e945bde04be7d564070806fddeb89231c9d81ec55ba8a4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Around Huayna Picchu the Urubamba River bends. The Incas built a trail up its side to temples and terraces at its top, 2,693 metres above sea level and high over Machu Picchu."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Urubamba River bends around Huayna Picchu [S1]; the Incas built a trail up its side [S4]; the Incas built temples and terraces at its top [S4]; its peak is 2,693 metres above sea level [S5]; it rises high over Machu Picchu [S3, S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Huayna Picchu is a mountain in Peru, beside Machu Picchu [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayna_Picchu]; The Urubamba River bends around Huayna Picchu [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayna_Picchu]; The Incas built a trail up the side of the mountain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayna_Picchu]; The Incas built temples at the top of the mountain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayna_Picchu]; The Incas built terraces at the top of the mountain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayna_Picchu]; The peak is 2,693 metres above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayna_Picchu]; The mountain rises high over Machu Picchu [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huayna_Picchu]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('59528a35-0132-4fee-926f-75c57fe53fb5'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the Maya cave of Actun Tunichil Muknal lies the Crystal Maiden, believed to be a 17-year-old boy, possibly a sacrifice, his bones calcified to a sparkling, crystallized appearance.', 'A Maya sacrificial cave with ceramics, stoneware, and skeletons still in place. The "Crystal Maiden" skeleton has calcified into sparkling crystalline form.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:59528a35-0132-4fee-926f-75c57fe53fb5:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A Maya sacrificial cave with ceramics, stoneware, and skeletons still in place. The "Crystal Maiden" skeleton has calcified into sparkling crystalline form.'' restored on Actun Tunichil Muknal', 'b79175423f93105c1e87e8edddcd3e352be136a5c52d23adce0b0589707caf3b|3e4b7dca3bb8f4c70cca1385be7880bf2613feef82d5e3453b425141e413b206|3e4b7dca3bb8f4c70cca1385be7880bf2613feef82d5e3453b425141e413b206', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Maya cave of Actun Tunichil Muknal lies the Crystal Maiden, believed to be a 17-year-old boy, possibly a sacrifice, his bones calcified to a sparkling, crystallized appearance."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Actun Tunichil Muknal is a Maya cave [S1]; the Crystal Maiden lies in it [S2, S3]; the Crystal Maiden is believed to be a 17-year-old boy [S3]; possibly a sacrifice victim [S3]; the bones are calcified to a sparkling, crystallized appearance [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Actun Tunichil Muknal is a cave in Belize that is a Maya archaeological site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Actun_Tunichil_Muknal]; The Crystal Maiden is a skeleton lying in the cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Actun_Tunichil_Muknal]; The Crystal Maiden is believed to be a 17-year-old boy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Actun_Tunichil_Muknal]; The Crystal Maiden was possibly a sacrifice [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Actun_Tunichil_Muknal]; The bones have calcified to a sparkling, crystallized appearance [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Actun_Tunichil_Muknal]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('597c2edf-5da7-48b5-9c32-466152182dac'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Beneath the lava of the Pedregal de San Ángel lies Cuicuilco, a city of pyramids, plazas and shallow pools, home to an estimated 20,000 at its peak and occupied until Xitle erupted.', 'A Mesoamerican center from 800 BC–250 AD, buried under a lava flow from Xitle volcano. The round pyramid still pokes through the hardened rock.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:597c2edf-5da7-48b5-9c32-466152182dac:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A Mesoamerican center from 800 BC–250 AD, buried under a lava flow from Xitle volcano. The round pyramid still pokes through the hardened rock.'' restored on Cuicuilco', '73c4221141737de3c8f82c0ba332f1de31bde2587db944c5b68ed8d5add9ec45|8885d7085e77841aac7c27bee8da4bcd1e3b3b9ec5331ad66742dc5c939cb681|8885d7085e77841aac7c27bee8da4bcd1e3b3b9ec5331ad66742dc5c939cb681', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Beneath the lava of the Pedregal de San Ángel lies Cuicuilco, a city of pyramids, plazas and shallow pools, home to an estimated 20,000 at its peak and occupied until Xitle erupted."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies beneath the lava of the Pedregal de San Ángel [S8]; it was a city with pyramids [S5]; it had plazas [S5]; it had shallow pools [S5]; its population at the peak is estimated at 20,000 [S6]; it was occupied until the eruption of Xitle [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cuicuilco is an archaeological site in Mexico City (Valley of Mexico), Mexico [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuicuilco]; Cuicuilco was a city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuicuilco]; The site lies beneath the lava of the Pedregal de San Ángel [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuicuilco]; The city had pyramids [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuicuilco]; The city had plazas and shallow pools [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuicuilco]; The population at its peak is estimated at 20,000 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuicuilco]; The site was occupied until the eruption of Xitle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuicuilco]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5a68414e-22b9-4e2e-a9b7-d2a46b25be9b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Once a grassy wetland drained in the 7th century BC, the Roman Forum became the centre of daily life in Rome: triumphal processions, elections and public speeches, now ringed by ruins.', 'The political heart of ancient Rome, a forum surrounded by temples, basilicas, and government buildings. Decisions made here shaped the Western world.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:5a68414e-22b9-4e2e-a9b7-d2a46b25be9b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The political heart of ancient Rome, a forum surrounded by temples, basilicas, and government buildings. Decisions made here shaped the Western world.'' restored on Roman Forum', 'ff70030672a50b4d1d70d3af984712457dc9a344129c0618bd535a65f33e153a|16a4c2a6db8cd8ef8019eea339ca78f6e089545cc77627855c9e25cd5ac09325|16a4c2a6db8cd8ef8019eea339ca78f6e089545cc77627855c9e25cd5ac09325', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Once a grassy wetland drained in the 7th century BC, the Roman Forum became the centre of daily life in Rome: triumphal processions, elections and public speeches, now ringed by ruins."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Roman Forum was originally a grassy wetland [S6]; it was drained in the 7th century BC [S6]; after the draining it became the centre of daily life in Rome [S3, S6]; triumphal processions took place there [S3]; elections took place there [S3]; public speeches were held there [S3]; it is now ringed by ruins [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-031 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Forum is a forum (a public plaza) in the centre of Rome, Italy, surrounded by ruins [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Forum]; The site of the Forum was originally a grassy wetland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Forum]; The wetland was drained in the 7th century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Forum]; The Forum became the centre of daily life in Rome [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Forum]; The Forum was the site of triumphal processions and elections [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Forum]; The Forum was the venue for public speeches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Forum]; Today the Forum is ringed by ruins [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Forum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5baef468-20dd-4e38-90ea-e209fc29e508'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Huánuco, Kotosh holds buildings from six periods of occupation, its Kotosh Period layer lying directly beneath the Chavin one. In its temple art, a signature image: crossed arms.', 'Kotosh is an archaeological site near the town of Huánuco consisting of a series of buildings comprising six periods of continuous occupation.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:5baef468-20dd-4e38-90ea-e209fc29e508:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Kotosh is an archaeological site near the town of Huánuco consisting of a series of buildings comprising six periods of continuous occupation.'' restored on Kotosh', 'c57845042f01b8d9c314769504dfd15c80425ef012661b316be5bd915d95a951|1ccb6a4f57d5b2a99c445577413b115300cb8f5a8f686db14d154bd93f439556|1ccb6a4f57d5b2a99c445577413b115300cb8f5a8f686db14d154bd93f439556', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Huánuco, Kotosh holds buildings from six periods of occupation, its Kotosh Period layer lying directly beneath the Chavin one. In its temple art, a signature image: crossed arms."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kotosh is near Huanuco [S1]; Kotosh holds buildings [S1]; the buildings come from six periods of occupation [S1]; the Kotosh Period layer lies beneath the Chavin layer [S2]; it lies directly beneath [S2]; the site has temple art/iconography [S7]; crossed arms is a signature (characteristic) image of that temple art [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-verify2-004 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kotosh is an archaeological site (a set of buildings) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotosh]; Kotosh is near Huánuco [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotosh]; Kotosh is in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotosh]; Kotosh holds buildings [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotosh]; The buildings come from six periods of occupation [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotosh]; Kotosh has a Kotosh Period layer (culture stratum) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotosh]; The Kotosh Period layer lies directly beneath the Chavin one [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotosh]; Kotosh has temple art (iconography) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotosh]; The image of crossed arms is characteristic of Kotosh temple art (its signature image) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotosh]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5c40d704-05a0-4951-a5cf-20066d444d81'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'A common with two saucer-shaped Bronze Age round barrows, now both a Scheduled Monument and an SSSI. The barrows are the low, dish-like type used for cremation burials.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:5c40d704-05a0-4951-a5cf-20066d444d81:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A common with two saucer-shaped Bronze Age round barrows, now both a Scheduled Monument and an SSSI. The barrows are the low, dish-like type used for cremation burials.'' restored on Walton Common', '||782de1c389657f4463cc8b90ec3a3cf15abf937001ac906d9cf1d8d2dfc7fe60', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: unproven-after-verify", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The web check could not prove: Walton Common is a place with prehistoric remains (a Scheduled Ancient Monument) near Walton in Gordano, England; The web check contradicts: There are two saucer-shaped Bronze Age round barrows on Walton Common (https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1007917?section=official-list-entry: \"The monument includes a slight univallate hillfort, two avenues, a saucer barrow, a regular aggregate field system\"); The web check contradicts: The enclosure on Walton Common is a banjo enclosure (https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1007917?section=official-list-entry: \"Incorrect suggestion of banjo encl\"); The web check could not prove: The enclosure dates to the late Iron Age; The web check could not prove: Walton Common has a Bronze Age round barrow (a saucer barrow); The web check could not prove: Saucer barrows are a form of round barrow"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5c7bd8b5-b6c8-4efa-be21-c84bfecdc3c7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the commune of Biguglia on Corsica, people lived at Monte Grossu in the Neolithic and Bronze Age. According to archaeologists, a village of 300 huts once stood here.', 'A small hill with Neolithic and Bronze Age layers, controlling a narrow passage between plain and lagoon. Its strategic position made it a natural chokepoint for thousands of years.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:5c7bd8b5-b6c8-4efa-be21-c84bfecdc3c7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A small hill with Neolithic and Bronze Age layers, controlling a narrow passage between plain and lagoon. Its strategic position made it a natural chokepoint for thousands of years.'' restored on Monte Grossu', '344bab29c35ef8acbd76ead108ad5db0f8741e3d4b1ffcea4c637d7445208b8f|70d6ad6bb8b3d2abb3f21371ed3c7c3b80fe5898ec7d25439f78cfa9ef0c79d6|70d6ad6bb8b3d2abb3f21371ed3c7c3b80fe5898ec7d25439f78cfa9ef0c79d6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the commune of Biguglia on Corsica, people lived at Monte Grossu in the Neolithic and Bronze Age. According to archaeologists, a village of 300 huts once stood here."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Monte Grossu is in the commune of Biguglia [S2]; it is on Corsica [S1]; people lived at the site in the Neolithic and Bronze Age [S3]; according to archaeologists a village of 300 huts stood there [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-031 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Monte Grossu is an archaeological site in the commune of Biguglia on Corsica [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Grossu]; People lived at Monte Grossu in the Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://www.corsematin.com/articles/biguglia-dans-les-secrets-de-la-presquile-123371]; People lived at Monte Grossu in the Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Grossu]; According to archaeologists, a village of 300 huts once stood at Monte Grossu [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monte_Grossu]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5d1b5446-41d9-46db-98c9-ac4d1996a3c1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Lough Gur, the Grange Stone Circle raises its largest stone, Rannach Chruim Duibh, over 4m high and weighing 40 tonnes. A second, smaller circle and a large leaning stone lie nearby.', 'The largest stone circle on the island, with its biggest stone weighing 40 tonnes and standing over 4 metres high. A second, smaller circle lies just to the northeast.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:5d1b5446-41d9-46db-98c9-ac4d1996a3c1:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The largest stone circle on the island, with its biggest stone weighing 40 tonnes and standing over 4 metres high. A second, smaller circle lies just to the northeast.'' restored on Grange Stone Circle', '7f949dc29f3ce108487b3f87c5080e13bcd1f44a2a21eb51496af9ebe4dc44d5|5d38f7aeeae034e6b403f55d8ecf0c9711971bd19c628188e77d9749920b6b0a|5d38f7aeeae034e6b403f55d8ecf0c9711971bd19c628188e77d9749920b6b0a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Lough Gur, the Grange Stone Circle raises its largest stone, Rannach Chruim Duibh, over 4m high and weighing 40 tonnes. A second, smaller circle and a large leaning stone lie nearby."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the circle is near Lough Gur [S2]; its largest stone is Rannach Chruim Duibh [S5]; the stone is over 4m high [S5]; the stone weighs 40 tonnes [S5]; a second, smaller circle lies nearby [S7]; a large leaning stone lies nearby [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-verify2-005 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Grange Stone Circle is a stone circle (the kind of place), in County Limerick, Ireland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grange_stone_circle]; The circle lies near Lough Gur [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grange_stone_circle]; Its largest stone is called Rannach Chruim Duibh [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grange_stone_circle]; The largest stone is over 4m high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grange_stone_circle]; The largest stone weighs 40 tonnes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grange_stone_circle]; A second, smaller circle lies nearby [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grange_stone_circle]; A large leaning stone lies nearby [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grange_stone_circle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('5d252b0e-96d1-4857-9883-72f508cabcd9'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Badshot Lea Long Barrow, the only known example in Surrey, was an earthen mound with a timber post in its eastern end. By the mid-1930s, chalk quarrying had destroyed much of its south.', '4th millennium BC long barrow in Surrey, built in the Early Neolithic. A communal tomb raised before the age of individual monuments — part of a burial tradition widespread across Neolithic Europe.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:5d252b0e-96d1-4857-9883-72f508cabcd9:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''4th millennium BC long barrow in Surrey, built in the Early Neolithic. A communal tomb raised before the age of individual monuments — part of a burial tradition widespread across Neolithic Europe.'' restored on Badshot Lea Long Barrow', '2a76869e07204cc1e4d2435a13d1f7505f824c23f63f175aa1a99c4b27774ca0|a7ed57c7f9c6bff95eecf8efd15a2b06f58845015079479a4c780291b671507c|a7ed57c7f9c6bff95eecf8efd15a2b06f58845015079479a4c780291b671507c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Badshot Lea Long Barrow, the only known example in Surrey, was an earthen mound with a timber post in its eastern end. By the mid-1930s, chalk quarrying had destroyed much of its south."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it is the only known example in Surrey [S2]; it was an earthen mound (built of earth, a tumulus) [S3, S4]; a timber post was embedded in the eastern end of the mound [S4]; by the mid-1930s chalk quarrying had destroyed much of its southern side [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-032 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Badshot Lea Long Barrow is a long barrow (a Neolithic burial mound) near Badshot Lea in Surrey, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Badshot_Lea_Long_Barrow]; It is the only known example of a long barrow in Surrey [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Badshot_Lea_Long_Barrow]; It was an earthen mound [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Badshot_Lea_Long_Barrow]; A timber post stood in the eastern end of the mound [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Badshot_Lea_Long_Barrow]; By the mid-1930s, chalk quarrying had destroyed much of its south [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Badshot_Lea_Long_Barrow]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('60212fa1-b2c0-4262-a434-adb4ade54b04'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The unexcavated Iron Age Posbury Hill Fort near Crediton partly encloses a hilltop 180 metres above sea level. W. G. Hoskins calls it a likely site of Posentesburg, a battle of 661 AD.', 'Iron Age hill fort at 180 m in Devon, unexcavated and bisected by a modern road. An incomplete earthwork enclosure holding undisturbed deposits never formally investigated by archaeologists.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:60212fa1-b2c0-4262-a434-adb4ade54b04:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Iron Age hill fort at 180 m in Devon, unexcavated and bisected by a modern road. An incomplete earthwork enclosure holding undisturbed deposits never formally investigated by archaeologists.'' restored on Posbury Hill Fort', '7b621ea59d9d9810f2c5bfea7edbff3d197ac50690e3f695f9d25acb59d49146|673a321b8219686f7d46304eb4255c3b28b5f54f66344afb3d31c444c8583238|673a321b8219686f7d46304eb4255c3b28b5f54f66344afb3d31c444c8583238', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The unexcavated Iron Age Posbury Hill Fort near Crediton partly encloses a hilltop 180 metres above sea level. W. G. Hoskins calls it a likely site of Posentesburg, a battle of 661 AD."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the hill fort is unexcavated [S1]; it is Iron Age [S1]; it is near Crediton [S1]; it partly encloses a hilltop [S2]; the hilltop is 180 metres above sea level [S2]; W. G. Hoskins calls it a likely site of Posentesburg [S4]; Posentesburg was a battle of 661 AD [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-032 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Posbury Hill Fort is a hill fort located near Crediton, Devon, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posbury]; The hill fort is unexcavated [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posbury]; The hill fort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posbury]; It partly encloses a hilltop [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posbury]; The hilltop is 180 metres above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posbury]; W. G. Hoskins calls it a likely site of Posentesburg [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posbury]; Posentesburg was a battle of 661 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posbury]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('608d793c-bd69-41e7-9330-17f35558f8d4'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On El Castro hill above El Burgo de Osma, Uxama Argaela was a Celtiberian, then Roman, city: walls with rectangular towers, cisterns, baths, and a basilica with mosaics.', 'A Celtiberian and Roman city that fought in the Celtiberian Wars, was conquered by Rome in 99 BC, then supported a rebel general before being destroyed by Pompey in 72 BC.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:608d793c-bd69-41e7-9330-17f35558f8d4:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A Celtiberian and Roman city that fought in the Celtiberian Wars, was conquered by Rome in 99 BC, then supported a rebel general before being destroyed by Pompey in 72 BC.'' restored on Uxama Argaela', 'f768254927dd125e35dc9925e823a71ea1411c2d54bdeb4472a0b6013c21f955|de06e0e2dac9cd7f9037cab0a533386c1d2a5a4d00ed4ad6a5d9a86ee277b27d|de06e0e2dac9cd7f9037cab0a533386c1d2a5a4d00ed4ad6a5d9a86ee277b27d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On El Castro hill above El Burgo de Osma, Uxama Argaela was a Celtiberian, then Roman, city: walls with rectangular towers, cisterns, baths, and a basilica with mosaics."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the city lay on El Castro hill [S1]; the hill overlooks El Burgo de Osma [S1]; Uxama Argaela was a Celtiberian and then Roman city [S1]; walls with rectangular towers [S3]; cisterns [S4]; baths [S4]; a basilica with mosaics [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-032 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Uxama Argaela was a city in Spain, on El Castro hill above El Burgo de Osma [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uxama_Argaela]; It was a Celtiberian city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uxama_Argaela]; It later became a Roman city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uxama_Argaela]; The city had walls with rectangular towers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uxama_Argaela]; The site has cisterns [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uxama_Argaela]; The site has baths [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uxama_Argaela]; The site has a basilica with mosaics [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uxama_Argaela]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('610ecf75-d2ea-4377-a26a-1f6b521bfcbc'::uuid, 'card_description', 'By the Eurotas at ancient Sparta, the Sanctuary of Artemis Orthia saw the éphēboi flogged; in Roman times, according to Cicero, the rite became a blood spectacle, sometimes to the death.', 'A sanctuary of Artemis Orthia, where Spartan boys endured ritual flogging to prove endurance. The Romans later built seating to watch.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:610ecf75-d2ea-4377-a26a-1f6b521bfcbc:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A sanctuary of Artemis Orthia, where Spartan boys endured ritual flogging to prove endurance. The Romans later built seating to watch.'' restored on Sanctuary of Artemis Orthia', '67edfd0d9bb1919006f514d91858713e559ea99a5a0ddacda214b3695445c388|d6df0c8ab737945e0455c5dffa69bddeff9c0c67d6d8106cd6055463ab17b3db|d6df0c8ab737945e0455c5dffa69bddeff9c0c67d6d8106cd6055463ab17b3db', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "By the Eurotas at ancient Sparta, the Sanctuary of Artemis Orthia saw the éphēboi flogged; in Roman times, according to Cicero, the rite became a blood spectacle, sometimes to the death."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the sanctuary lies by the Eurotas River [S2]; it lies at ancient Sparta [S2]; the cult of Orthia involved the flogging of the éphēboi [S4]; in Roman times the ritual became a blood spectacle [S5]; this is according to Cicero [S5]; the spectacle was sometimes to the death [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-032 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Sanctuary of Artemis Orthia is a sanctuary at ancient Sparta [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_of_Artemis_Orthia]; The sanctuary lies by the Eurotas River [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_of_Artemis_Orthia]; At the sanctuary the éphēboi were flogged (the diamastigosis) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_of_Artemis_Orthia]; In the Roman period the rite changed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_of_Artemis_Orthia]; According to Cicero, the rite became a blood spectacle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_of_Artemis_Orthia]; The blood spectacle was sometimes to the death [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_of_Artemis_Orthia]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('617627d7-010d-4aa2-a9da-0d3160855f8a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Guarding the Rhiangoll pass at 450 m, Castell Dinas has the highest castle in England and Wales, a hillfort where a hall-keep seems to have risen within curtain walls and square towers.', 'A fortified Norman castle with stone walls was built on the site possibly by William Fitz Osbern or his son Roger de Breteuil, Earl of Hereford in the period 1070 to 1075.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:617627d7-010d-4aa2-a9da-0d3160855f8a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A fortified Norman castle with stone walls was built on the site possibly by William Fitz Osbern or his son Roger de Breteuil, Earl of Hereford in the period 1070 to 1075.'' restored on Castell Dinas', '0d1013f74f00b388517a17a6f4158ef536e7286c4f268d160d593566e1504f8a|236d014a54623c17e1afc46c6f7a19d89503dc88b2fa2d7ac365845f74421bbf|236d014a54623c17e1afc46c6f7a19d89503dc88b2fa2d7ac365845f74421bbf', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Guarding the Rhiangoll pass at 450 m, Castell Dinas has the highest castle in England and Wales, a hillfort where a hall-keep seems to have risen within curtain walls and square towers."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "it guards (is positioned to defend) the Rhiangoll pass [S3]; it stands at 450 m [S2]; it has the highest castle in England and Wales [S2]; it is a hillfort [S1]; a hall-keep seems to have been built (hedged) [S5]; the hall-keep was surrounded by curtain walls and square towers [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-032 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Castell Dinas is a hillfort and castle in southern Powys, Wales [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castell_Dinas]; It guards the Rhiangoll pass [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castell_Dinas]; It stands at 450 m [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castell_Dinas]; It has the highest castle in England and Wales [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castell_Dinas]; The site was a hillfort (Iron Age) on which the castle was built [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castell_Dinas]; A hall-keep seems to have risen within curtain walls and square towers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castell_Dinas]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('61f1e287-c3bc-40ad-b3fe-af956af4cb1b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Deep in dense woodland near Falkirk lies Tappoch Broch, its paved oval chamber centred on a large fire spot, a short aisle leading to an unusually well-preserved staircase in the wall.', 'Tappoch Broch, better known as Torwood Broch, is an iron-age broch located in a remote spot in dense woodland near Falkirk.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:61f1e287-c3bc-40ad-b3fe-af956af4cb1b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Tappoch Broch, better known as Torwood Broch, is an iron-age broch located in a remote spot in dense woodland near Falkirk.'' restored on Tappoch Broch', '07c418539ec3dec27bd11ded298e17da362d8b7af20e90cde30f6bea4671870d|aeb851f36762bc848687757378c15cb6b4ed70204642c1711d2abbdca3e39211|aeb851f36762bc848687757378c15cb6b4ed70204642c1711d2abbdca3e39211', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Deep in dense woodland near Falkirk lies Tappoch Broch, its paved oval chamber centred on a large fire spot, a short aisle leading to an unusually well-preserved staircase in the wall."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the broch lies in dense woodland [S1]; the spot is remote (deep in the woodland) [S1]; it is near Falkirk [S1]; the oval chamber is paved [S4]; the chamber has a large fire spot in the middle [S4]; a short aisle leads to a staircase in the wall [S5]; the staircase is unusually well preserved [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-033 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tappoch Broch is a broch (an iron-age fortified dwelling) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tappoch_Broch]; It is near Falkirk (Scotland) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tappoch_Broch]; It lies deep in dense woodland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tappoch_Broch]; Its chamber is oval [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tappoch_Broch]; The chamber is paved [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tappoch_Broch]; The chamber is centred on a large fire spot [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tappoch_Broch]; A short aisle branches off the chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tappoch_Broch]; The short aisle leads to a staircase in the wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tappoch_Broch]; The staircase is unusually well preserved [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tappoch_Broch]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('62baaf4c-8ad4-4bf4-b0e0-e5587c5a64a1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Hidden in the copse crowning Goffers Knoll lies a Bronze Age bowl barrow, 21m in diameter. Without the trees it would be a prominent landmark overlooking the ancient Icknield way.', 'Hidden within the copse of trees which surmounts the knoll is a bowl barrow which dates from the Bronze Age and is reported to be substantially intact.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:62baaf4c-8ad4-4bf4-b0e0-e5587c5a64a1:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Hidden within the copse of trees which surmounts the knoll is a bowl barrow which dates from the Bronze Age and is reported to be substantially intact.'' restored on Goffers Knoll', '7bf4430c0e393dc8c93046a2379bdd4e88ca4c89422d5ddf05cfe5f79ea43d4f|e666f8627ad4f2a7a56126b009fef50438f78018c145a59f59677d99afb57722|e666f8627ad4f2a7a56126b009fef50438f78018c145a59f59677d99afb57722', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Hidden in the copse crowning Goffers Knoll lies a Bronze Age bowl barrow, 21m in diameter. Without the trees it would be a prominent landmark overlooking the ancient Icknield way."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "a copse of trees crowns (surmounts) the knoll [S2]; a barrow is hidden within the copse [S2]; the barrow is a Bronze Age bowl barrow [S2]; the barrow is 21m in diameter [S4]; without the trees the barrow would be a prominent landmark [S5]; it overlooks the ancient Icknield way [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-033 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Goffers Knoll holds a bowl barrow (a burial mound) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goffers_Knoll]; The bowl barrow dates from the Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goffers_Knoll]; The barrow is hidden in a copse of trees [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goffers_Knoll]; The copse crowns (surmounts) the knoll [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goffers_Knoll]; The barrow is 21 m in diameter [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goffers_Knoll]; Without the trees the barrow would be a prominent landmark [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goffers_Knoll]; The barrow overlooks the ancient Icknield Way [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goffers_Knoll]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('630f8251-632b-4924-8ec9-3cacec046732'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Govurqala, a town and cemetery of circa 40 ha in Boyahmadly village, is estimated to have been the summer residence of Caucasus Albania rulers and to hold remnants of the town of Aluen.', 'A 3rd–10th-century town with a Byzantine coin of Emperor Anastasius I among its finds. Researchers link it to the summer residence of Caucasus Albania''s rulers and the medieval town of Aluen.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:630f8251-632b-4924-8ec9-3cacec046732:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description "A 3rd–10th-century town with a Byzantine coin of Emperor Anastasius I among its finds. Researchers link it to the summer residence of Caucasus Albania''s rulers and the medieval town of Aluen." restored on Govurqala, Ağdam', 'ded96ec6b5bf1d4221617049c32fac8874f4604f7a5e81be3a4f77474da8e646|fcd169d81e3916297294b878066e29ea33eb179c7c9bb169dee4ed1b08b3eb2e|fcd169d81e3916297294b878066e29ea33eb179c7c9bb169dee4ed1b08b3eb2e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-011", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Govurqala, a town and cemetery of circa 40 ha in Boyahmadly village, is estimated to have been the summer residence of Caucasus Albania rulers and to hold remnants of the town of Aluen."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-011 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Govurqala is a town and cemetery [S1]; it covers circa 40 ha [S1]; it lies in Boyahmadly village [S1]; it is estimated to have been the summer residence of Caucasus Albania rulers [S2]; it is estimated to hold remnants of the town of Aluen [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-033 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.