    ('681b6f74-7b5c-4c1a-99b4-0f58822e5e39'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In a meander of the Tampaón River lies Tamtoc, a Huastec city where 90% of the burials found so far are women, and most clay figurines depict women thought to have held high rank.', 'Until now, at least three periods of occupation have been identified: the oldest occurred around 600 BC.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:681b6f74-7b5c-4c1a-99b4-0f58822e5e39:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Until now, at least three periods of occupation have been identified: the oldest occurred around 600 BC.'' restored on Tamtoc', 'c8ec29de3dfc9f6aa26a8d7b7dd5bfa3b2309b8e612fbe5e651cc988a3fe4588|ee9b1cd0b5b918a732e2279144a463b06bf73543e63885c5f022840244af7009|ee9b1cd0b5b918a732e2279144a463b06bf73543e63885c5f022840244af7009', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In a meander of the Tampaón River lies Tamtoc, a Huastec city where 90% of the burials found so far are women, and most clay figurines depict women thought to have held high rank."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tamtoc lies in a meander of the Tampaón River [S5]; Tamtoc is a Huastec city [S1, S5]; 90% of the burials found so far are women [S3]; most of the clay figurines depict women [S4]; the women are thought to have held high rank [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-035 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tamtoc is a Huastec city (an archaeological site of the Huastec culture) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamtoc]; Tamtoc lies in a meander of the Tampaón River [SUPPORTED: https://lugares.inah.gob.mx/en/node/5500]; 90% of the burials found so far at Tamtoc are women [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamtoc]; Most of the clay figurines found at Tamtoc depict women [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamtoc]; The women are thought to have held high rank [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamtoc]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('68677772-b461-49a3-bf2b-376e2b0e970b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Crescent dunes up to 40 m high curve beside the dry bed of Lake Mungo. Among its most publicized finds: Mungo Woman, only partially cremated before the rest of her bones were crushed.', 'The first evidence of human occupation in Lake Mungo was revealed after an excavation of two Lake Mungo trenches contained 11 silcrete flakes.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:68677772-b461-49a3-bf2b-376e2b0e970b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The first evidence of human occupation in Lake Mungo was revealed after an excavation of two Lake Mungo trenches contained 11 silcrete flakes.'' restored on Lake Mungo', 'cc58fbeac6678364ddef53ad16be9e3d176b7047df4f141d08894f4592d4fecb|d8106073978394a1f56e7ca1578cda36e43ea734b81a946f3135dfa2a1ad6956|d8106073978394a1f56e7ca1578cda36e43ea734b81a946f3135dfa2a1ad6956', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Crescent dunes up to 40 m high curve beside the dry bed of Lake Mungo. Among its most publicized finds: Mungo Woman, only partially cremated before the rest of her bones were crushed."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "there are crescent-shaped dunes at Lake Mungo [S4]; the dunes are up to 40 m high [S4]; the dunes lie beside the bed of Lake Mungo [S4]; Lake Mungo is a dry lake [S1]; Mungo Woman is among the most publicized finds [S5]; Mungo Woman was only partially cremated [S6]; the remainder of her bones were crushed [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-035 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Lake Mungo is a dry lake in Australia (New South Wales) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Mungo]; The dunes at Lake Mungo are crescent-shaped [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Mungo]; The crescent dunes are up to 40 m high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Mungo]; The crescent dunes lie beside the lake bed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Mungo]; The lake bed is dry [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Mungo]; Mungo Woman is among the most publicized finds at Lake Mungo [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Mungo]; Mungo Woman was only partially cremated [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Mungo]; The rest of Mungo Woman''s bones were crushed after the partial cremation [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Mungo]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('68ff480b-a02b-4458-b131-22dff4a00df6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Once named Amenemhet is Mighty, this mud brick pyramid at Dahshur was built on clay that could not support its weight, and it began to sink. Its dark rubble mound named it the Black Pyramid.', '1860–1814 BC Black Pyramid of Amenemhat III at Dahshur — the first to house both pharaoh and queens together. Originally called Amenemhet is Mighty, it earned its dark name from centuries of decay.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:68ff480b-a02b-4458-b131-22dff4a00df6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''1860–1814 BC Black Pyramid of Amenemhat III at Dahshur — the first to house both pharaoh and queens together. Originally called Amenemhet is Mighty, it earned its dark name from centuries of decay.'' restored on The Black Pyramid- Pyramid of Amenemhat III (Dahshur)', 'e600dda637419ea5573dd6567d32122e60d8fd1d44369103a42e1d96d23b0dae|76dfd1e34e237a9461c20672c09c6f59b1d91f975457214111beb62c985ee91b|76dfd1e34e237a9461c20672c09c6f59b1d91f975457214111beb62c985ee91b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Once named Amenemhet is Mighty, this mud brick pyramid at Dahshur was built on clay that could not support its weight, and it began to sink. Its dark rubble mound named it the Black Pyramid."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the pyramid was originally named Amenemhet is Mighty [S3]; the pyramid is made of mud brick [S7]; the pyramid is at Dahshur [S2]; it was built on clay [S8]; the clay could not support its weight [S8]; the pyramid began to sink [S8]; its dark rubble mound gave it the name Black Pyramid [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-035 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Black Pyramid (Pyramid of Amenemhat III) is a pyramid at Dahshur [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Pyramid]; Dahshur is in Egypt [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Pyramid]; The pyramid is a mud brick pyramid [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Pyramid]; The pyramid was once named Amenemhet is Mighty [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Pyramid]; It was built on clay [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Pyramid]; The clay could not support the pyramid''s weight [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Pyramid]; The pyramid began to sink [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Pyramid]; Its dark rubble mound appearance gave it the name Black Pyramid [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Pyramid]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6b6a62fc-a743-47a2-9caf-9d41ee6a36e7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In Cornwall, The Pipers, two standing stones in separate fields about 90 metres apart, point almost directly at The Merry Maidens. An excavation in 1871 brought no result.', 'The Pipers are on a northeast to southwest alignment which points almost directly at The Merry Maidens. The stones were first recorded by William Borlase in 1754.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:6b6a62fc-a743-47a2-9caf-9d41ee6a36e7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The Pipers are on a northeast to southwest alignment which points almost directly at The Merry Maidens. The stones were first recorded by William Borlase in 1754.'' restored on The Pipers, St Buryan', '8e1290df01c8a10aa818115cfeaf3631dbb3516e7028ea799d26e32aadc02866|95ed2fcc4a2f8990b01d47269fe320a4a051cf66af035e344424c3c65ff33bba|95ed2fcc4a2f8990b01d47269fe320a4a051cf66af035e344424c3c65ff33bba', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Cornwall, The Pipers, two standing stones in separate fields about 90 metres apart, point almost directly at The Merry Maidens. An excavation in 1871 brought no result."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the stones are in Cornwall [S1]; the Pipers are two standing stones [S1]; they stand in separate fields [S3]; they are about 90 metres apart [S3]; their alignment points almost directly at The Merry Maidens [S2]; an excavation took place in 1871 [S7]; the excavation brought no result [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-verify2-005 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Pipers are two standing stones (the kind of place), a pair [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pipers,_St_Buryan]; The Pipers are in Cornwall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pipers,_St_Buryan]; The two stones stand in separate fields [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pipers,_St_Buryan]; The two stones are about 90 metres apart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pipers,_St_Buryan]; The stones point almost directly at The Merry Maidens [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pipers,_St_Buryan]; The Merry Maidens is a Late Neolithic stone circle (the target the stones point at) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merry_Maidens]; The stones were excavated in 1871 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pipers,_St_Buryan]; The 1871 excavation brought no result [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Pipers,_St_Buryan]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6b808340-302a-4866-9c2d-46391aff4ce3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the river Queiles, Tarazona was Turiaso, a prosperous Roman city of full Roman citizens. The Visigoths called it Tirasona, and in the 8th century it became a Muslim town.', 'Known as Roman Turiaso, this city passed through Visigothic, Muslim, and Christian rule. A textbook example of layered history in a single place.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:6b808340-302a-4866-9c2d-46391aff4ce3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Known as Roman Turiaso, this city passed through Visigothic, Muslim, and Christian rule. A textbook example of layered history in a single place.'' restored on Tarazona', 'ed54423e3755a2689d2d91a23053daca7f3edc8a1ae34509579b60bfcfd30d57|218559eef3792aadc4ec3d5dee7d1b23261a1c4acfad2b2146a0215c417bbc3f|218559eef3792aadc4ec3d5dee7d1b23261a1c4acfad2b2146a0215c417bbc3f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the river Queiles, Tarazona was Turiaso, a prosperous Roman city of full Roman citizens. The Visigoths called it Tirasona, and in the 8th century it became a Muslim town."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tarazona lies on the river Queiles [S1]; Tarazona was Turiaso [S1, S3]; it was a prosperous Roman city [S3]; its inhabitants were full Roman citizens [S3]; the Visigoths called it Tirasona [S2]; in the 8th century it became a Muslim town [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-036 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Tarazona is a town (a settlement) in Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarazona]; Tarazona lies on the river Queiles [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarazona]; Tarazona was called Turiaso in Roman times [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarazona]; Roman Turiaso was a prosperous Roman city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarazona]; The inhabitants of Roman Turiaso were full Roman citizens [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarazona]; The Visigoths called the city Tirasona [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarazona]; In the 8th century the city became a Muslim town [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarazona]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6bb12b0e-f96b-4243-a96f-395cc7c5d317'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Cut into the rock at Karli, the Great Chaitya of the Karla Buddha Caves is the largest rock-cut chaitya in India, 45 metres long, its hall carved with men, women, lions and elephants.', 'Ancient Buddhist rock-cut caves with large windows carved into a hillside to light the interiors. The complex spans the 2nd century BC to the 5th century AD.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:6bb12b0e-f96b-4243-a96f-395cc7c5d317:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Ancient Buddhist rock-cut caves with large windows carved into a hillside to light the interiors. The complex spans the 2nd century BC to the 5th century AD.'' restored on Karla Buddha Caves', '7979c35bd970a233b796d9c7af727d15835bb50bc0cb5dbd7c7d096464097f74|6a0f334096f3720e0b10db3b2bddb98fddff154ebaf38b08c2136eff43dcb2a1|6a0f334096f3720e0b10db3b2bddb98fddff154ebaf38b08c2136eff43dcb2a1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cut into the rock at Karli, the Great Chaitya of the Karla Buddha Caves is the largest rock-cut chaitya in India, 45 metres long, its hall carved with men, women, lions and elephants."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the caves are cut into the rock [S1]; the site is at Karli [S1]; the Great Chaitya is part of the Karla Buddha Caves [S1, S5]; it is the largest rock-cut chaitya in India [S6]; it is 45 metres long [S6]; its hall is carved with men and women [S7]; its hall is carved with lions and elephants [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-036 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Karla Buddha Caves are a complex of ancient Buddhist caves at Karli, India [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karla_Caves]; The caves at Karli are cut into the rock (rock-cut) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karla_Caves]; The Great Chaitya is the main cave of the Karla caves (Cave No. 8) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karla_Caves]; The Great Chaitya is the largest rock-cut chaitya in India [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karla_Caves]; The Great Chaitya is 45 metres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karla_Caves]; The hall is carved with men (male figures) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karla_Caves]; The hall is carved with women (female figures) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karla_Caves]; The hall is carved with lions [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karla_Caves]; The hall is carved with elephants [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karla_Caves]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6c3149ff-3881-4a25-bd83-167325c511ce'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At Petsofas in eastern Crete, thousands of clay figurines were left in a small enclosure: likely worshippers, sacrificial animals, and human limbs thought to plead for divine healing.', 'A Minoan peak sanctuary where unique clay figurines of weasels and tortoises were found alongside the usual human and animal offerings. No other peak sanctuary has these.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:6c3149ff-3881-4a25-bd83-167325c511ce:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A Minoan peak sanctuary where unique clay figurines of weasels and tortoises were found alongside the usual human and animal offerings. No other peak sanctuary has these.'' restored on Petsofas', '22cdd4a75db3d8696d70daf2f18b34794b0c5f820cac7dd4e5efd7f812eae30c|587dcff0e155d10d2e1befb10e8c28dc887869ebcda4a069e1dc335c24dfb249|587dcff0e155d10d2e1befb10e8c28dc887869ebcda4a069e1dc335c24dfb249', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Petsofas in eastern Crete, thousands of clay figurines were left in a small enclosure: likely worshippers, sacrificial animals, and human limbs thought to plead for divine healing."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Petsofas is in eastern Crete [S1]; thousands of clay figurines were left [S2]; they were left in a small enclosure [S2]; the figurines are likely worshippers [S4]; the figurines include sacrificial animals [S4]; the figurines include human limbs [S4]; the limbs are thought to be associated with requests for divine healing [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-036 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Petsofas is an archaeological site in eastern Crete [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petsofas]; The site consisted of a small enclosure [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petsofas]; Thousands of figurines were left in the enclosure [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petsofas]; The figurines are of clay [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petsofas]; The figurines include human figures that likely represent worshippers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petsofas]; The figurines include sacrificial animals [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petsofas]; The figurines include human limbs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petsofas]; The human limbs are thought to be associated with requests for divine healing [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petsofas]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6ca3b4c8-5cd6-485c-9290-d24b2caca0c0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Chichén Itzá, in eastern Yucatán, was one of the largest Maya cities, its name meaning "At the mouth of the well of the Itzá". The Temple of Kukulcán dominates its North Platform.', 'At its peak between 750 and 900 AD, Chichen Itza drew the most culturally diverse population in the Maya world. That mix of peoples produced its remarkable variety of architectural styles.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:6ca3b4c8-5cd6-485c-9290-d24b2caca0c0:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''At its peak between 750 and 900 AD, Chichen Itza drew the most culturally diverse population in the Maya world. That mix of peoples produced its remarkable variety of architectural styles.'' restored on Chichén Itzá', '5f7fef3dff6f9f306e607442c79b66f3c11a9062580d5cc1f412eec5a9e06b7c|39a5a01448199bae3c2ba0dd913fbcc3ce7b14778ccdd0d47f2948b613baa679|39a5a01448199bae3c2ba0dd913fbcc3ce7b14778ccdd0d47f2948b613baa679', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chichén Itzá, in eastern Yucatán, was one of the largest Maya cities, its name meaning \"At the mouth of the well of the Itzá\". The Temple of Kukulcán dominates its North Platform."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chichen Itza lies in eastern Yucatan [S3]; it was one of the largest Maya cities [S7]; its name means ''At the mouth of the well of the Itza'' [S2]; the Temple of Kukulcan dominates the North Platform [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-verify2-005 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chichén Itzá was a Maya city (the kind of place), a large one [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichen_Itza]; Chichén Itzá is in eastern Yucatán [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichen_Itza]; It was one of the largest Maya cities [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichen_Itza]; Its name means \"At the mouth of the well of the Itzá\" [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichen_Itza]; The Temple of Kukulcán dominates its North Platform [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichen_Itza]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6d2b0b49-565b-41ed-8763-aa0f35d7c627'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Buried beneath later constructions, the Roman Theatre of Caesaraugusta, 106 meters across and one of the largest in Roman Hispania, stayed hidden until excavations in the 1970s.', 'The theater''s decline began in the 3rd century when stones were repurposed to fortify the city walls and other constructions.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:6d2b0b49-565b-41ed-8763-aa0f35d7c627:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description "The theater''s decline began in the 3rd century when stones were repurposed to fortify the city walls and other constructions." restored on Roman Theatre, Zaragoza', 'c6c2329e8a3166629fb767e564515a15ebfa9f9f89a2510c48720bbdc6c7e852|032e406490e695bc2f99bc27c788a20fa74f0d557fb09e74027130a8760f5099|032e406490e695bc2f99bc27c788a20fa74f0d557fb09e74027130a8760f5099', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-012", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Buried beneath later constructions, the Roman Theatre of Caesaraugusta, 106 meters across and one of the largest in Roman Hispania, stayed hidden until excavations in the 1970s."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-012 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the theatre was buried beneath later constructions [S7]; it is the Roman theatre of Caesaraugusta [S1, S2]; it is 106 meters across [S4]; it is one of the largest theatres in Roman Hispania [S4]; it stayed hidden until the 1970s [S8]; excavations in the 1970s brought it back to light [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-036 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Theatre of Caesaraugusta is a Roman theatre in Zaragoza, Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theater_(Zaragoza)]; The theatre was buried beneath later constructions [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theater_(Zaragoza)]; The theatre is 106 meters across [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theater_(Zaragoza)]; The theatre is one of the largest in Roman Hispania [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theater_(Zaragoza)]; The theatre stayed hidden (obscured) until excavations [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theater_(Zaragoza)]; The excavations that uncovered the theatre took place in the 1970s [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theater_(Zaragoza)]; The theatre was uncovered by archaeological excavations in 1973 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Theater_(Zaragoza)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6d9aac40-25c6-4379-8cc8-175b1a33f894'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Under its ring of limestone voussoirs, the road still runs through Newport Arch, a 3rd-century Roman gate in Lincoln, reputedly the oldest arch in the United Kingdom used by traffic.', 'A 3rd-century Roman arch in Lincoln that is still used by traffic today. It is the oldest arch in the UK that vehicles still drive through.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:6d9aac40-25c6-4379-8cc8-175b1a33f894:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A 3rd-century Roman arch in Lincoln that is still used by traffic today. It is the oldest arch in the UK that vehicles still drive through.'' restored on Newport Arch', 'aa4968bc96f1770e7331b2f8d7c787a6b79e07f87cde8fdc6a860528d71b5411|434423e4e03a08868e7401c32e544bf9f8a58bcea61ca66240a7ffd1655ead3b|434423e4e03a08868e7401c32e544bf9f8a58bcea61ca66240a7ffd1655ead3b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-013", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Under its ring of limestone voussoirs, the road still runs through Newport Arch, a 3rd-century Roman gate in Lincoln, reputedly the oldest arch in the United Kingdom used by traffic."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-013 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is called Newport Arch [S1]; Newport Arch is a Roman gate [S1]; Newport Arch dates to the 3rd century [S1]; Newport Arch is in Lincoln [S1]; its arch is a ring of limestone voussoirs [S6]; the road still runs through the arch (it is still used by traffic) [S2, S4]; it is reputedly the oldest arch in the United Kingdom still used by traffic [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-037 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Newport Arch is a Roman gate [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newport_Arch]; The gate is in Lincoln [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newport_Arch]; The gate dates to the 3rd century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newport_Arch]; It is reputedly the oldest arch in the United Kingdom used by traffic [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newport_Arch]; The road still runs through the arch (it is still used by traffic) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newport_Arch]; The gate carries a road (it was the north gate on the Roman road Ermine Street) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newport_Arch]; The arch is a ring of voussoirs (a single ring) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newport_Arch]; The voussoirs are of limestone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newport_Arch]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6f502f33-3251-4054-b334-4fa52ea7b671'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Trottiscliffe in Kent, the Coldrum Long Barrow rose from earth and around fifty sarsen megaliths; its stone chamber held the remains of at least seventeen men, women and children.', 'Built of sarsen — the same stone used at Stonehenge — this Neolithic chambered long barrow is part of the Medway Megaliths cluster, a dense concentration of prehistoric tombs in Kent.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:6f502f33-3251-4054-b334-4fa52ea7b671:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Built of sarsen — the same stone used at Stonehenge — this Neolithic chambered long barrow is part of the Medway Megaliths cluster, a dense concentration of prehistoric tombs in Kent.'' restored on Coldrum Long Barrow', 'b8461884927f6be54c1927a04c2d1efed5618d48e8aa627a7ac6788c7d5969df|df903dd23f735776d85ff2d260f001bdb0019bf1392cc5895dad73c1cb5811b8|df903dd23f735776d85ff2d260f001bdb0019bf1392cc5895dad73c1cb5811b8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-013", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Trottiscliffe in Kent, the Coldrum Long Barrow rose from earth and around fifty sarsen megaliths; its stone chamber held the remains of at least seventeen men, women and children."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-013 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is the Coldrum Long Barrow [S1]; it is near Trottiscliffe [S1]; it is in Kent [S1]; the barrow was built of earth [S4]; it was built with around fifty sarsen megaliths [S4]; it had a stone chamber [S5]; the chamber held human remains [S5]; the remains are of at least seventeen individuals [S6]; the individuals were men, women and children [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-037 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Coldrum Long Barrow is a chambered long barrow (a tomb) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]; It is near Trottiscliffe [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]; Trottiscliffe is in Kent [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]; It was built of earth [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]; It was built of around fifty sarsen megaliths [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]; It had a stone chamber [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]; The chamber held human remains [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]; The remains were of at least seventeen people [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]; The remains included men [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]; The remains included women [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]; The remains included children [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coldrum_Long_Barrow]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6f6c5cc0-b0ac-4222-bb04-747352eb83d1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In Pompeii''s Garden of the Fugitives lie casts of 13 victims of 79 AD, adults and children caught by the pyroclastic flow, apparently while trying to find a way out of the Nocera Gate.', 'The plaster casts of 13 people killed by Vesuvius in 79 AD, frozen mid-flight in a garden that was a vineyard before the eruption. A family with small children is among them.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:6f6c5cc0-b0ac-4222-bb04-747352eb83d1:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''The plaster casts of 13 people killed by Vesuvius in 79 AD, frozen mid-flight in a garden that was a vineyard before the eruption. A family with small children is among them.'' restored on Garden of the Fugitives, Pompeii', 'bb0000a53861f75b6b29cf363ed49bca1d988dd7821d38be9bfcd8c3392fe494|bc25a4072018f535d858dbe8f312ebfb0ade521090c02a41d5455dc5a578fa53|bc25a4072018f535d858dbe8f312ebfb0ade521090c02a41d5455dc5a578fa53', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-013", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Pompeii''s Garden of the Fugitives lie casts of 13 victims of 79 AD, adults and children caught by the pyroclastic flow, apparently while trying to find a way out of the Nocera Gate."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-013 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Garden of the Fugitives is in Pompeii [S1]; the site contains casts of victims [S2]; there are 13 cast victims [S2]; the victims died in the eruption of 79 AD [S2]; the victims were adults and children [S5]; they were caught by the pyroclastic flow [S6]; they apparently died while trying to find a way out of the Nocera Gate (hedge kept) [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-037 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Garden of the Fugitives is an archaeological site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_the_Fugitives]; The Garden of the Fugitives is in Pompeii [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_the_Fugitives]; It contains casts of victims [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_the_Fugitives]; There are 13 victim casts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_the_Fugitives]; The victims died in the eruption of 79 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_the_Fugitives]; The victims were adults and children [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_the_Fugitives]; The victims were caught by the pyroclastic flow [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_the_Fugitives]; They apparently died while trying to find a way out [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_the_Fugitives]; The way out they were seeking was the Nocera Gate [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garden_of_the_Fugitives]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6fc1cbb2-3926-4ef2-b59f-0e3e4337fd45'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Considered among the world''s oldest continuously inhabited places, the Amman Citadel holds the Great Temple, better but inaccurately known as the Temple of Hercules.', 'The Amman Citadel is considered to be among the world''s oldest continuously inhabited places.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:6fc1cbb2-3926-4ef2-b59f-0e3e4337fd45:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description "The Amman Citadel is considered to be among the world''s oldest continuously inhabited places." restored on Amman Citadel', 'c779425bf269c1b846aa2aa77d99b61d55b7d1ad63bbd9d046343c3046fbb53b|b21ccb31fb9f86f0e2d02a7beb9f88226d0d5045a23928c5e9e67c47b6d6fb47|b21ccb31fb9f86f0e2d02a7beb9f88226d0d5045a23928c5e9e67c47b6d6fb47', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-013", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Considered among the world''s oldest continuously inhabited places, the Amman Citadel holds the Great Temple, better but inaccurately known as the Temple of Hercules."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-013 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Amman Citadel is considered among the world''s oldest continuously inhabited places (hedge kept) [S1]; the Great Temple is within the Amman Citadel [S3]; the Great Temple is better known as the Temple of Hercules [S3]; the name Temple of Hercules is inaccurate [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-037 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Amman Citadel is an archaeological site on a hill in Amman, Jordan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amman_Citadel]; It is considered among the world''s oldest continuously inhabited places [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amman_Citadel]; The Citadel holds the Great Temple (Temple of Hercules) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amman_Citadel]; The Great Temple is better but inaccurately known as the Temple of Hercules [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amman_Citadel]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7054234a-ec07-477d-afe0-df191dbca303'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a Wingate sandstone cliff in Indian Creek Canyon, Newspaper Rock State Historic Monument holds over 650 rock art designs: deer, buffalo, pronghorn, human figures and symbols.', 'People of the Archaic, Basketmaker, Fremont, and Ancestral Puebloan cultures carved petroglyphs on Newspaper Rock from around the beginning of the current era until about 1300.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:7054234a-ec07-477d-afe0-df191dbca303:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''People of the Archaic, Basketmaker, Fremont, and Ancestral Puebloan cultures carved petroglyphs on Newspaper Rock from around the beginning of the current era until about 1300.'' restored on Newspaper Rock State Historic Monument', '8f221e985c0ff974d72a40c393eb3251f94581325a2b46d107c3a5097f6b78ff|a2372c2ed3718db5d7f9471d66d981c3f6642336a03fea84ae814f5daee7de3a|a2372c2ed3718db5d7f9471d66d981c3f6642336a03fea84ae814f5daee7de3a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-013", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a Wingate sandstone cliff in Indian Creek Canyon, Newspaper Rock State Historic Monument holds over 650 rock art designs: deer, buffalo, pronghorn, human figures and symbols."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-013 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the rock is on a Wingate sandstone cliff [S3]; it is in Indian Creek Canyon [S3]; there are over 650 rock art designs [S6]; the designs include deer, buffalo and pronghorn [S8]; the designs include human figures and symbols [S7]; the site is Newspaper Rock State Historic Monument [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-037 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Newspaper Rock State Historic Monument is a state monument (USA, Utah) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper_Rock_State_Historic_Monument]; It features a rock panel of rock art (petroglyphs) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper_Rock_State_Historic_Monument]; The rock is part of a Wingate sandstone cliff [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper_Rock_State_Historic_Monument]; The cliff is in Indian Creek Canyon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper_Rock_State_Historic_Monument]; It holds over 650 rock art designs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper_Rock_State_Historic_Monument]; The designs include deer [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper_Rock_State_Historic_Monument]; The designs include buffalo [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper_Rock_State_Historic_Monument]; The designs include pronghorn [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper_Rock_State_Historic_Monument]; The designs include human figures [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper_Rock_State_Historic_Monument]; The designs include symbols [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newspaper_Rock_State_Historic_Monument]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('70ba6d0f-f026-4b29-9379-a9152ee1e271'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Fin Cop''s Iron Age hill fort in Monsal Dale shows signs of hurried building. Excavation unearthed a mass burial of only women and children, probably massacred after the fort was attacked.', 'Fin Cop is a hill and an associated Iron Age hill fort in Monsal Dale, close to Ashford in the Water in Derbyshire.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:70ba6d0f-f026-4b29-9379-a9152ee1e271:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''Fin Cop is a hill and an associated Iron Age hill fort in Monsal Dale, close to Ashford in the Water in Derbyshire.'' restored on Fin Cop', '42cac913d1a17b4c8585844289aa34115b331e3461db0a16e731d9e67a8d5f8d|a4a26ece45ddc0b36f72fe37c16ab9e8468cffd96887b58182bd0b890e008ebb|a4a26ece45ddc0b36f72fe37c16ab9e8468cffd96887b58182bd0b890e008ebb', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Fin Cop''s Iron Age hill fort in Monsal Dale shows signs of hurried building. Excavation unearthed a mass burial of only women and children, probably massacred after the fort was attacked."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Fin Cop has an Iron Age hill fort [S1]; the fort is in Monsal Dale [S1]; the fort shows signs of hurried building [S2]; excavation unearthed a mass burial [S3]; the burial held only women and children [S3]; they were probably massacred (hedge kept) [S3]; the massacre came after the fort was attacked [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-verify2-005 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Fin Cop has a hill fort (the kind of place), in Monsal Dale [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fin_Cop]; The hill fort is of the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fin_Cop]; The fort shows signs of hurried building [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fin_Cop]; Excavation unearthed a mass burial [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fin_Cop]; The mass burial contained only women and children [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fin_Cop]; The victims were probably massacred [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fin_Cop]; The massacre came after the fort was attacked [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fin_Cop]; Fin Cop is in England (Derbyshire) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fin_Cop]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('70c04f92-593e-467b-8568-b585e1cdb58f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Long Meg and Her Daughters lies Little Meg, a small circle of large kerb stones, one still bearing concentric circles and a spiral; at its centre a cist held bones and charcoal.', 'A small circle of kerb stones that once surrounded a Bronze Age cairn. One stone bears rare spiral and concentric ring carvings — a type of art more common in Ireland than here.', 'teaser-card-s018-rollback:70c04f92-593e-467b-8568-b585e1cdb58f:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s018: card_description ''A small circle of kerb stones that once surrounded a Bronze Age cairn. One stone bears rare spiral and concentric ring carvings — a type of art more common in Ireland than here.'' restored on Little Meg', '118b67abd5f406a34a534bfa7650a245b76c54514c5d694d5cedcc974ac23e53|2b5ffefb4ff8b94a2a0f82250e9c50c15ab7ef033589e5856626bc01d5f25914|2b5ffefb4ff8b94a2a0f82250e9c50c15ab7ef033589e5856626bc01d5f25914', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-write-013", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Long Meg and Her Daughters lies Little Meg, a small circle of large kerb stones, one still bearing concentric circles and a spiral; at its centre a cist held bones and charcoal."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-check-013 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Little Meg is near Long Meg and Her Daughters [S2]; Little Meg is a small circle of large kerb stones [S1]; one stone still bears concentric circles and a spiral [S4]; a cist lies at the centre of the circle [S6]; the cist held bones and charcoal [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-06: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-06-verify-038 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-06/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Little Meg is a